WEBVTT

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.742
Gaussian splatting describes a scene with
hundreds of thousands of 3D Gaussians.

00:00:04.742 --> 00:00:08.516
Each has a centre, a shape, an opacity and
a colour.

00:00:08.516 --> 00:00:11.395
To render, every Gaussian is projected to
the image,

00:00:11.396 --> 00:00:13.942
and the splats are blended from front to
back.

00:00:13.941 --> 00:00:15.661
Every step is differentiable,

00:00:15.662 --> 00:00:19.692
so all the parameters are fitted to
photographs by gradient descent.

00:00:19.691 --> 00:00:25.529
Unlike NeRF, no network is queried along
each ray, so rendering runs in real time.

00:00:25.482 --> 00:00:30.344
A gradient step on the raw covariance
could produce a matrix that isn't a
covariance at all.

00:00:30.344 --> 00:00:34.219
So splatting stores a rotation and three
scales instead.

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:39.857
Sigma is R S S transpose R transpose,
which is valid by construction.

00:00:39.857 --> 00:00:43.557
Spin the rotation, and the same long
Gaussian turns.

00:00:43.557 --> 00:00:47.332
At ninety degrees, its long axis points
along y.

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:49.143
The pinhole camera isn't linear,

00:00:49.144 --> 00:00:53.586
so splatting linearises it at the centre
of each Gaussian, with the Jacobian.

00:00:53.585 --> 00:00:57.791
A linear map sends covariance Sigma to J
Sigma J transpose,

00:00:57.792 --> 00:01:00.073
the rule the Kalman filter uses.

00:01:00.072 --> 00:01:04.535
On the axis, at depth two, this gives a
round splat of five pixels.

00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:09.710
Off the axis, the depth extent is seen at
an angle, and the splat widens sideways.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.047
Twice as far, and it's half the size.

00:01:12.061 --> 00:01:16.486
Red has opacity zero point five, and blue
has zero point eight.

00:01:16.486 --> 00:01:19.424
Now blend them in the wrong order, with
blue in front.

00:01:19.424 --> 00:01:20.986
Does the pixel change?

00:01:20.986 --> 00:01:23.074
Does the light that gets through change?

00:01:23.074 --> 00:01:24.636
Pause and decide.

00:01:26.097 --> 00:01:29.160
The colour changes, but the light left
over does not.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.885
Red in front gives a mix of red and blue.

00:01:31.885 --> 00:01:34.210
In the wrong order, it's mostly blue.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.647
Yet one tenth of the light gets through
either way:

00:01:36.647 --> 00:01:39.797
the product doesn't care about order, but
the colour does.

00:01:39.797 --> 00:01:43.522
So real renderers sort splats by depth,
tile by tile.

00:01:43.522 --> 00:01:46.873
And during training, Gaussians are cloned,
split or pruned,

00:01:46.874 --> 00:01:49.373
so the detail goes where the scene needs
it.
